## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where content is PROVIDED to all 236 useContent consumers. It currently creates a STATIC context value from defaultContent via useMemo — content never updates after initial render. For Live Preview to deliver draft content in real-time, this provider must subscribe to Contentstack Live Preview SDK events and update the content state when they fire. All downstream consumers (useContent → getContentByKey) read from this single context, so fixing the provider fixes delivery to every consumer.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo with useState(initialized from defaultContent) so content can be updated at runtime. Add a useEffect that, only when running inside the CMS editor iframe (detect via Contentstack Live Preview SDK's isLivePreviewEnabled or window.__CONTENTSTACK_LIVE_PREVIEW__), calls the SDK's onLivePreview callback to setState with the incoming draft content. The provider already receives defaultContent and wraps it in context — the only change is making that context value mutable via live-preview events instead of frozen via useMemo. The useContent hook and all 236 consumers require zero changes because they already read from ContentstackContext.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When the application page is opened inside the Contentstack CMS editor's preview panel, the rendered page displays the draft (unpublished) version of the content being edited, not the last published version.
- When the application page is opened outside the CMS editor (e.g., in a normal browser tab), the rendered page displays the published content, with no draft or unpublished changes visible.
- While the page is open in the CMS editor's preview panel, when a content author edits a text field in the CMS, the edited text appears on the rendered page without the author publishing the entry or manually reloading the page.
- While the page is open in the CMS editor's preview panel, when a content author makes several successive edits to content, each successive change becomes visible on the rendered page, reflecting the latest edit.
- When a content author edits content that appears in multiple places on the rendered page, every visible instance of that content on the page shows the updated draft text.
- When the page is open in the CMS editor's preview panel and the live connection to the CMS is lost (e.g., network interruption), the rendered page continues to display the most recently shown content without crashing, going blank, or showing an error screen.
- When the page is open in the CMS editor's preview panel and the author switches between different entries or content items in the CMS, the rendered page updates to show the content of the item currently being edited.

